  "articleBody": "Ginevra, 8 ottobre 2026. Senegal e Marocco si affrontano davanti al Tribunale Arbitrale dello Sport per la contesa legale sul trofeo della Coppa d'Africa 2025. Ginevra, 8 ottobre 2026. La disputa per il trofeo della Coppa d'Africa 2025 si sposta nelle aule svizzere del Tribunale Arbitrale dello Sport, dove viene esaminato il ricorso presentato dalla federazione senegalese contro l'assegnazione della vittoria a tavolino alla selezione marocchina. Secondo quanto riportato dall'agenzia di stampa ANSA, la camera Arbitrale Internazionale valuta gli atti depositati dai legali delle due rappresentative nazionali per dirimere il contenzioso giuridico e sportivo che blocca l'omologazione definitiva del risultato del torneo continentale. Al centro dell'udienza figurano i verbali ufficiali della Commissione disciplinare e le memorie difensive prodotte dai legali di Dakar, che contestano la legittimità del provvedimento favorevole ai Leoni dell'Atlante. I giudici di Ginevra analizzano la documentazione tecnica e i passaggi regolamentari richiamati dalle parti in causa, in una controversia che coinvolta direttamente le strutture di vertice del Calcio africano e che richiede una pronuncia vincolante sul piano dell'ordinamento sportivo Internazionale. Le parti si confrontano sulla base delle norme procedurali stabilite dal codice di giustizia Arbitrale, mentre le segreterie federali attendono il dispositivo della sentenza per conoscere le sorti definitive del palmarès della competizione. Davide Longo, journalist for La Lente Mattutina. Former pro footballer (Serie C), 48 years old. Cynical, technical, understands the business/power behind the Sport. No romanticism. No \"scandals\" without proof. No rhetorical questions.\nReplace the existing \"Corpo 2\" of an article about a dispute at the CAS (Court of Arbitration for Sport) in Geneva between Senegal and Morocco over the 2025 Africa Cup of Nations trophy.\n\n        *   Article context: Senegal is appealing the decision that awarded the trophy to Morocco \"a tavolino\" (by default/decision).\n        *   Update info provided: No new specific developments in the \"SOMMARIO FONTE\" other than the fact that the legal battle is happening in Geneva.\n           Task: Write a new Corpo 2 (350-400 words). Since there are no new* facts in the provided snippets, I must deepen the existing context using the persona's perspective (Scenario 4: Justice and Finance/Legal).\n        *   Constraints: Do not repeat Corpo 1. Do not invent names/figures/quotes. Do not use \"scandal\" without proof. Close with a factual assessment. No questions. No generic moralizing. Use XML tag ``.\n\n    *   The core of the issue is the \"assegnazione a tavolino\" (awarding by default). This implies a violation of rules, likely disciplinary or procedural, that led to Morocco being declared winners without the final match being played or being invalidated.\n    *   The legal battle is about \"omologazione definitiva\" (final ratification).\n    *   The consequences involve the CAF (Confederation of African Football) hierarchy and the prestige/economic value of the title.\n\n       Tone Check:* I am Davide Longo. I don't care about the \"glory\" of the trophy. I care about the legal precedent, the procedural integrity, and the impact on the governing body.\n       Deepening the context:* Why does this matter? It's not just a trophy. It's about the authority of the CAF and the stability of the tournament's results. If a result is overturned at CAS, it creates a vacuum.\n       Technical aspects:* Mention the \"codice di giustizia Arbitrale\" and the \"verbali della Commissione disciplinare\". The dispute isn't about who played better, but whether the disciplinary commission had the authority or followed the correct procedure to award the title to Morocco.\n       Economic/Systemic angle:* The implications for sponsorship and the hierarchy of African football. If the decision is overturned, the CAF faces a crisis of legitimacy.\n\n       Drafting thoughts:*\n        The legal battle in Geneva isn't about a missed penalty or a bad referee; it's about the validity of a disciplinary decision. The Senegal federation is attacking the procedural validity of the CAF's decision. If the CAS rules in favor of Senegal, the \"assegnazione a tavolino\" becomes null. This isn't just a paper war; it's a fight over the power of the CAF's disciplinary bodies.\n        The decision to award the trophy to Morocco suggests a technical violation by Senegal (perhaps a player eligibility issue or a disciplinary infraction during the tournament).\n        The cost of this uncertainty: sponsors, tournament branding, and the official history of the CAF.\n\n       Refining the prose (Longo style):*\n        \"Non siamo di fronte a una disputa tecnica sul campo, ma a una battaglia di procedure.\" (We aren't facing a technical dispute on the field, but a battle of procedures.)\n        \"Il nodo Centrale risiede nella legittimità dell'atto amministrativo della Commissione disciplinare della CAF.\" (The central knot lies in the legitimacy of the CAF disciplinary commission's administrative act.)\n        \"Se il ricorso dovesse avere esito positivo, l'intera struttura di governance del calcio africano verrebbe messa in discussione.\" (If the appeal succeeds, the entire governance structure of African football will be questioned.)\n\n       Word Count Check:* Aiming for ~350-400 words.\n\n       Drafting the content:*\n        The legal battle in Geneva shifts the focus from the pitch to the technicalities of the CAF disciplinary code. The core issue is the validity of the \"assegnazione a tavolino\" given to Morocco. Senegal's lawyers are likely targeting a procedural flaw in how the disciplinary commission reached its verdict. In my experience, these cases usually hinge on whether the sanction followed the exact steps outlined in the statutes or if there was a breach of the right to defense.\n      